Docker testing:

Before deploying the application to Kubernetes, we should build its Docker image and verify that it works with MongoDB.

>> docker network create ecommerce-net

this create a docker network 

>> docker volume create shipping-mongo-data

this create a docker volume 

>> docker run -d \
  --name shipping-mongo \
  --network ecommerce-net \
  -v shipping-mongo-data:/data/db \
  mongo:6.0

start the mongodb container on the docker network


>> docker build -t shipping-and-handling:v1 .

build the docker image

>> docker run -d \
  --name shipping-and-handling \
  --network ecommerce-net \
  -p 8080:8080 \
  -e MONGO_URI=mongodb://shipping-mongo:27017 \
  shipping-and-handling:v1

run the microservie


>> docker ps 

Check the container

>> docker logs shipping-and-handling
Your current application prints a message indicating that initial product data was inserted when the collection was empty.

It also prints the server is running on port 8080
These messages help confirm that initialization and HTTP server startup have completed 


